in this lesson we're going to talk about  systems of equations now in the act this  is an important concept to understand  and there's a few different ways to  solve these systems of equations you can  graph them and find out where the two  lines intersect  or you can use the substitution or  elimination method so let's look at a  couple examples here together and then  we'll get into some of the word problems  as you would likely see them on the ect  so this first example here is really set  up nicely to use the substitution method  so how do we do the substitution method  well we solve for one of the variables  in this case we know what y equals it's  3x plus 1 and what we can do is we can  substitute just like a substitute  teacher you can swap that out with your  normal teacher and  put that in place of y here so we have  2x minus and notice i'm putting that in  parentheses that whole quantity is being  subtracted  equals negative 4. now we just have one  equation with x in it that's going to be  easy to solve so first thing i'm going  to do is i'm going to distribute the  negative so we get 2x minus 3x minus 1  equals negative four  and then that's going to give us  negative x minus one equals negative  four add one to the other side that  gives us negative x equals negative  three and you can see that uh by  multiplying both sides by negative one x  is three  now how do we solve for y well you can  take that x value and put it either in  here or in here either one and solve for  y you're going to get the correct answer  the same answer but it's going to be  easier to put into this one because we  already have y by itself so if we put 3  in here we get y equals three  times three plus one so that means y  equals ten so three comma ten  is the point where the two lines are  going to cross or intersect now if it's  a multiple choice question sometimes  you're just better off and it's easier  and quicker just to take those solutions  a b c d  and go ahead and put them in and see  which ones are going to make both  equations true now you want to make sure  it doesn't just make one of them true it  has to make them both true then you know  that's the common point of intersection  so you can work backwards in that manner  i'm just showing you how to do the  substitution method just the algebraic  method okay now another approach to  these problems is to do the elimination  method and this is handy when both of  the variables are on the left the  numbers are on the right x's and y's are  lined up and what you want to do is you  want to add these equations together or  subtract these equations in such a way  that either the y's cancel out or the  x's cancel out sometimes what you'll  have to do is you'll have to multiply  one of the equations by a constant maybe  let's say by let's say if i wanted to  eliminate x's here i'd have to multiply  this by negative 2 negative 2 negative  2. so when i add those the x's would  cancel out but in this case i can see  i've got negative y and positive y i  could just add them as they are right  now and the y's are going to cancel so  2x plus 1x is 3x  the y's cancel we get 8 plus 7 is 15.  divide both sides by 3  x is 5. if i put 5 back into here or  here i can solve for y i'm just going to  put it into this bottom equation  five plus y equals seven subtract five  from both sides y equals two so the  answer is going to be five comma two now  again if i want to check my answer just  put five and two in for x and y in both  equations as long as it makes them both  true you know you got the right answer  okay so let's get into some of the more  uh  likely forms of the system of equation  problems as you would see them on the  act so go ahead and pause the video see  if you can do these problems on your own  we'll go through them together  i'll read it here for you if you have  two dollars and you save ten dollars per  week and your brother has sixty dollars  and saves twelve dollars per week and  how many weeks we have the same amount  okay so we wanna do is we wanna write  two equations and the way we would do  that is we'll just use the slope  intercept form of the line y equals mx  plus b y represents how much you've  saved  b represents the amount that you have  currently that's the initial condition  and the and the slope that's the rate at  which you're saving so here we have  let's see  let's see you've got y equals  10 x plus  72 that's for you and your brother would  be let's see y equals 12x plus 60. so he  already has 60 that's his initial  condition and his rate of saving as 12  dollars per week now we're trying to  solve for x the number of weeks at which  point you'll both have the same amount  this one's a good one to do substitution  we know what y equals let's just put  that in place of y there and so now what  we have is 10x plus 72 equals 12x plus  60.  i'm going to subtract 10x from both  sides  so that gives us 2x plus 60 equals 72.  i'm going to subtract 60 from both sides  so that's 12 equals 2x and divide both  sides by 2 and you can see x equals 6 so  in 6 weeks you'll have the same amount  okay example 2 it says if 3x plus 5y  equals 13 and 2x minus 4y equals  negative 6 what is the value of x plus  3y  so see if you can do this one on your  own and we'll do it together i'm just  going to write it down here 3x plus 5y  equals  let's see 13 right  and we've got 2x minus 4y equals  negative 6.  okay so in this case here what i'm going  to do is i'm going to try to eliminate  the the x's and i'm going to do that by  multiplying the top equation by negative  2 and the bottom equation by 3. now why  am i doing that well negative 2 times 3  is negative 6x 3 times 2 gives you  positive 6x this way when we add those  together the x's are going to cancel out  and we can solve for y  so let me go ahead and do that i'm going  to distribute the negative 2 to  everything here and that's going to give  us negative 6x  right  minus 10y  equals negative 26. and if we multiply  here everything by 3 we get let's see  positive 6x  minus 12y  equals  negative 18. okay so when we add you can  see the x's cancel we get negative 22 y  equals  negative 44 divided by negative 22 and  you can see that y equals 2.  all we have to do now is put the 2 back  into any one of these equations for y  and solve for x i'm just going to put it  into the top equation so that's 3x plus  5 times 2 equals 13.  3x plus 10 equals 13. subtract 10 from  both sides 3x equals 3 divide by 3 and  you can see that x equals 1 so our  solution is 1 comma 2  and again if you want to see if that's  the right answer you can just put 1 and  2 in you know and make sure that you get  13  and also negative 6. but now we have to  take this one step further it says  what's the value of x plus 3y  well that's going to be 1 plus 3 times 2  so that's 6 plus 1 equals 7. so your  final answer is going to be 7. they just  added on an extra step here  once we found the solution we had to  just substitute those values in so again  substitution elimination and solving  systems of equations is an important  concept to understand for the act

